From 2246ad7ea60fff8696ea5e99fe2a3a975fe060ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Intel MPSS <mpss@intel.invalid>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 18:16:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 03/22] i386: KNC vector and mask register moves

From Intel MPSS 3.8.6: make the 512-bit vector registers (as xmm0-31)
and the mask registers k0-k7 usable for scalar values on KNC.

KNC has no SSE: a scalar in a vector register is loaded with a
broadcast (vpbroadcastd/q, vbroadcastss/sd, vbroadcasti32x4) and stored
with a masked vpackstorel{d,q,ps,pd} under a mask in k1/k2 (knc.md).
Stores are routed through ix86_secondary_reload and ix86_expand_move.
Misaligned SF/DF/DI/TF moves and the TImode input reload go through a
stack slot and an integer scratch.  Mask constants are loaded via a
free (or saved) GPR after reload (ix86_split_load_mask_reg, needing
ix86_save_reg_1).  k2 is reserved as the post-reload store mask.  KNC
spells kmovw "kmov" and has no k-register logic.  The new operand code
'J' prints a memory operand without its size.

KNC's kmov only moves between mask and general registers, so the HI/QI
mask moves to and from memory are disabled for KNC: the mixed "k <- km"
alternative of *movhi_internal is split into "k <- k" and a new last
"k <- m", and a spilled mask value is reloaded through a general
register.

The mask-constant ("CBC") alternatives GCC 9 added to *movhi_internal
and *movqi_internal are disabled for KNC, which has no k-register logic.

Intel's "x", "v" and "Yv" constraints also admit the KNC vector
registers, so upstream alternatives that rely on them implying SSE are
gated with isa noknc (or the new x64_noknc): the zeroing ("C")
alternatives of the scalar move patterns, the pshufd split of
*highpartdisi2 and the punpcklqdq split of *concat<mode><dwi>3_3.

Differences from Intel's code:
 - The operand code is 'J'; Intel used 'M', now taken for the x32 VSIB
   addr32 prefix.
 - The misaligned-move patterns carry their stack slot and scratch as
   real clobbers of the insn; Intel had them inside the UNSPEC, where
   they are not definitions, and IRA turned the scratch into a pseudo
   without a live range (heap corruption building libgomp).
 - The stack slot is a pseudo that the register allocator has to put in
   memory: a fixed frame slot as clobber operand was eliminated with a
   stale offset when the prologue saved registers, and the copy
   overwrote other locals.  The slot of the TImode input reload is a
   KNC-own SLOT_KNC_TEMP, as SLOT_TEMP is gone.
 - Intel allowed kmov with a memory operand, which the k1om assembler
   rejects.
 - The SImode register copy prints its operands with %g (zmm names);
   Intel used %1/%0, which print xmm names the k1om assembler does not
   know.
 - An empty misaligned-DImode block (its body was commented out) and a
   whitespace-only change in ix86_expand_move are dropped.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Anselm Busse <anselm.busse@outlook.com>
